course: adult-life
lesson: 56
title: Scary films, nightmares and sad endings
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Films are made to make us feel things: scared, sad, excited, happy. The film is pretend, but the feelings it makes are real, and they can stay with you after the screen goes dark.
explain: When a film scares you, your body acts as if the danger were real: your heart races, your hands get sweaty, you jump. Part of your brain knows you are safe on the sofa, and that mix is why some people enjoy it and others really don't.
explain: Vivid example: in Manchester, Freya comes home from a sleepover yawning. She whispers to her mum that the girls watched a scary film and she lay awake till three, listening to every creak. Mum doesn't laugh. "Lots of people feel like that after a scary film, love. Let's leave the landing light on tonight, and next time you can just say it's not for you."
explain: Sad films work the same way: a sad ending can make you cry or keep you thinking for days, and that is normal. Grown-ups choose films by asking two questions: is it right for my age, and am I in the mood for this feeling tonight?
fact: A scary film makes the body react as if the danger were real, with a racing heart and quick breathing, even though you are safe.
fact: Many people enjoy being scared by a film because it is a thrill with no real danger, like a roller coaster; other people simply don't, and both are fine.
fact: Nightmares after a scary film are common; they fade after a few nights, and talking about the film or leaving a light on helps.
fact: Scary music is used to build suspense: it tells you something might happen before anything does.
fact: A jump scare is a sudden picture or loud sound meant to make you jump; it works even in cartoons and even when you know it is coming.
fact: Trailers show the most exciting or scary bits squeezed together, so a trailer can feel scarier than the whole film.
fact: Hiding behind a cushion or switching off halfway is a sensible choice, not cowardice; being brave means doing what is right for you, not proving something.
fact: Some films are made to look like real news or real recordings, but they are still stories; a real alien landing would be on every proper news channel.
fact: "Based on a true story" means the film starts from something real, but film makers change, add and invent a lot to make it exciting.
fact: Sad films can make us cry or think for days, especially when a sad event reminds us of our own life, such as our mum or a pet or a hard time at school.
fact: Some films are slow on purpose; grown-ups may love a quiet film that children find boring, and that is a difference in taste, not a fault.
fact: Rewatching a scary film often makes it less scary, because you know what is coming and your body stops being surprised.
fact: Old black-and-white films were made before colour film was common; many are still loved and are worth watching with someone who remembers them.
fact: Staying up late for the end of a film steals sleep, and tired children are often grumpy and slow the next day.
exercise: Freya watched a scary film at a sleepover and couldn't sleep afterwards. What is going on? | A normal reaction to a scary film, which will fade | A sign that something is badly wrong with Freya | Proof that the film was secretly real all along | A sign she is too old for films now
exercise: Alfie wakes from a nightmare about the monster film. "Will it keep happening forever?" What's true? | Nightmares after a scary film fade after a few nights | Nightmares last for as long as the film is still on at the cinema | Nightmares only stop if he watches it again tonight | Nightmares mean the monster is hiding in the house
exercise: Grace: "The trailer was scarier than I thought!" Why can a trailer feel so scary? | It squeezes the scariest bits close together | It is always made by a different film maker | Trailers are louder by law than films are | Trailers show the parts cut out of the film
exercise: Jake hides behind a cushion and his brother laughs. Is hiding a silly thing to do? | No, it's a sensible way to take a break from fear | Yes, only very little children ever do that | Yes, he should stare at the screen to toughen up | No, but only because the cushion blocks the sound
exercise: Ruby wants to watch a scary film "to prove I'm brave". What would a good parent tell her? | Being brave isn't about proving it with a film | Watching scary films is the only way to become really brave | Brave people never feel scared by anything | She should watch two to be twice as brave
exercise: Harry jumps when a monster suddenly bangs on a window in a cartoon. What is this called? | A jump scare, a sudden picture or loud sound | A trailer, a short advert shown before the film | A plot twist, a surprise turn in the story | A sequel, the second part of a film
exercise: Poppy watched a film that looked like real news about aliens landing. "Was it real?" | No, it was a story made to look like news | Yes, it was real, the news showed it last night | Nobody knows, scientists are still checking | Yes, but only in another country
exercise: Max: "Why do people like being scared by films?" What's the best answer? | It's a thrill with no real danger, like a roller coaster | They don't know it's going to be a scary film until it starts | Being scared makes people grow taller and stronger | They watch so they can learn to fight monsters
exercise: Sophie can't stop thinking about a sad film where a mum dies. What helps most? | Talking about it with someone she loves | Watching the sad ending over and over again | Pretending to everyone she never saw the film | Never watching any film ever again
exercise: Theo yawns through the slow, sad film his parents love. What is going on? | A difference in taste; slow films aren't for everyone | The film is broken and should go back to the shop | His parents are pretending to like it to annoy him | He is ill, because everyone loves slow films
exercise: Lily watches a film where a girl is bullied and it reminds her of school. What would a good parent do? | Ask her about it and listen | Tell her to stop being so dramatic | Switch off and never mention it | Ring the film company to complain
exercise: Oscar laughs at the scary film and his friend thinks he's weird. What's true? | People react to fear in different ways, laughing too | Laughing at a film means he isn't really watching it | Only bad people laugh at scary films, so he must be one | Laughing makes the film scarier for everyone
exercise: Evie watched a film where a dog gets lost and runs to check on her cat. Why? | The film made her feel worried about her own pet | The film told her that her own cat was lost | Cats always run away after a sad film | She thinks the film was secretly about her own cat
exercise: Finn: "It's based on a true story, so all of it happened." Put him right. | Some of it is real, but much was changed or invented | He's right, every word happened exactly so, just as shown | None of it happened, the phrase is a lie | Only the music is from the true story
exercise: After a sad film about a hurt horse, Ava says she wants to be a vet. What has happened? | The film made her feel something and gave her an idea | The film has made her choose her job for the rest of her life | The film was an advert for vet schools | The film has secretly taught her to be a vet
exercise: Leo stayed up to see the end of a film and is grumpy at breakfast. Why? | He didn't get enough sleep | The film had a bad ending | Films make people grumpy | He ate too much popcorn
exercise: Mia wants to rewatch a scary film to make it less scary. Can that work? | Yes, knowing what comes takes away the surprise | No, a film gets scarier and scarier each time you see it | Yes, because the film changes every time | No, the scary bits are cut the second time
exercise: Isla: "Why's the music creepy when nothing's happening?" | It builds suspense, telling you something may happen | The musicians made a mistake in that part of the film | It's there to cover a noise on the set | It means the film is about to finish
test: Noah's grandad turns on an old film with no colour at all. "Is the telly broken, Grandad?" What's the truth? | No, it's a black-and-white film from long ago | Yes, the colour part of the telly has stopped working | No, it's a film about a world with no colour | Yes, old films only work on old tellies
test: Ella switches off a Halloween film halfway. Her mate: "omg ur such a chicken lol." Who's right? | Ella, stopping when it's too much is sensible | Her mate, you have to finish every film you start | Her mate, switching off halfway is against the rules | Nobody, they should have watched the sequel instead
test: Big brother to little sister: "Jump scares only work if you don't know they're coming, so I'm not scared." Put him right. | Jump scares often work even when you expect them | He's right, they never work at all if you know they're coming | Jump scares only work on very young children | Jump scares only work if the sound is off
test: Mum on the phone, after the sleepover: "Freya's fine, just a bit shaky, she didn't sleep a wink after that film." What does "didn't sleep a wink" mean? | She hardly slept at all | She slept with her eyes open | She slept all day long instead | She winked at everybody she saw
test: Dad grumbles: "Bloody trailer before a kids' film, scared her half to death." What is he cross about? | A trailer was too scary for the children watching | His daughter was badly hurt by something at the cinema | The kids' film itself was far too long | The trailer gave away the ending of the film
test: A boy on the bus: "That alien film was real, my cousin says it was on the news." What is the best check? | Look at a proper news channel; it would be everywhere | Believe the cousin, because cousins always know these things | Watch the film again to see if it's true | Ask the boy to swear it is true on his life
test: Your sister: "Crying at a sad film is so embarrassing." Put her right. | Crying at a sad film is normal, even for grown-ups | Only babies cry at sad films, so she's right to say it | Crying means you didn't understand the film | Crying at films is bad for your eyes
test: Aunt, laughing: "I still can't watch that shark film, and I'm forty!" What does this show? | Grown-ups get scared by films too | The film must have been all about her | Sharks only scare people over forty | She is lying to make you feel better
test: Leo begs: "Pleeease let me watch the end, it's only an hour!" It's already late on a school night. What would a good parent say? | Watch the end tomorrow; sleep matters on a school night | Yes, an hour makes no difference to anyone, even at bedtime | Yes, but only if you skip breakfast tomorrow | No, and you can never watch that film again
test: Sophie, weeks later: "I keep thinking about the mum in that film. Does that mean my mum will die?" What's the kind, true answer? | No, the film is a story; thinking about it is normal | Yes, films often tell us what will happen next in real life | Nobody knows, so you'd better keep watching it | No, mums in real life never get ill at all
test: Gran to Theo, who's sulking: "You don't have to love it, love. Go and read your book." What does she mean? | It's fine not to like the film they enjoy | He must sit there until he learns to like the film | Reading is his punishment for not liking it | Books and films are exactly the same thing
exam: Identify the principal reason for which an individual's heart rate may increase during the viewing of a frightening film. | The body reacts as though the danger depicted were real | The film transmits a signal which directly affects the heart | The viewer is in genuine physical danger throughout the film | The room is invariably heated to a high degree during such a viewing
exam: Determine the meaning of the phrase "based on a true story" as employed in respect of a cinematographic work. | That the work derives from real events but alters them | That every event depicted occurred precisely as shown | That the events depicted are entirely fictitious | That the work was filmed at the actual time of the events
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the repeated viewing of a frightening film. | It commonly diminishes fear, the content being anticipated | It invariably augments fear on each successive occasion | It is prohibited for persons under eighteen years of age | It renders the depicted events real in the mind of the viewer
exam: Whereas a child has terminated the viewing of a frightening film prior to its conclusion, ascertain the assessment which this lesson commends. | A sensible decision, being right for that child | An act of cowardice warranting correction | A breach of the rules governing sleepovers | An error requiring the film to be restarted from the beginning
